    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="35.1">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:IICn">II Cn</ref> 35.1</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="35.1" corresp="#wit-g-a">Witodlice, se ðe freondleasan ⁊ feorran cumenan wyrsan dom demeð þonne his geferan, he dereð him sylfum.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="35.1" corresp="#wit-a">Witodlice, se ðe freondleasan ⁊ feorran cumenan wyrsan dom demeð þonne his geferan, he derað hym sylfum.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="35.1" corresp="#wit-q">Scienter, qui amicis destituto uel extraneo deterius iudicium iudicat quam socio suo, se ipsum ledit.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#robertson" n="35.1" corresp="#wit-b">Witodlice, se ðe freondleasan ⁊ feorran cumenan wyrsan dóm demeð ðonne his geferan, he derað him sylfum.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-b">Verily, he who pronounces a more severe judgment upon one who is friendless or come from afar than upon one of his own acquaintances injures himself.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann">Wahrlich, wer einem Sippenlosen und fernher Gekommenen schlimmeres Urtheil zuerkennt als seinem Genossen, der schädigt sich selbst.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">Truly, whoever gives a worse judgment to a man without kin and one come from afar than to his own fellow harms himself.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">Liebermann compares Af El. 33, 47; I Cn 5.2a–c.</note>
      </div>
    </body>
